string closeAlterationBatch(string payloadJson)
{
	// API for the Production Supervisor Widget. Custom API, POST, one argument.
	//
	// CLOSES AN ALTERATION BATCH by DECLARATION. This is the whole reason the
	// function exists, so it is worth saying plainly:
	//
	// An alteration batch has no continuous quantity flow. One garment can be
	// worked at two stages - a seam redone AND its embroidery redone - so the
	// per-stage Qty_Out figures cannot be summed into a batch total without
	// knowing which garments overlap, and nobody records that. Adding them would
	// count the same garment twice; taking the maximum would silently drop the
	// ones that only needed one stage. There is no arithmetic that recovers it.
	//
	// So attrition is DECLARED ONCE, here, at the end - not derived from the
	// stages. The supervisor is asked "how many garments are going back for
	// checking?", defaulted to the batch size and reducible only, and that number
	// is Qty_Produced. Qty_Ordered minus it is Qty_Lost.
	//
	// ALTERATION STAGE LOGS ARE WORK RECORDS, NOT ATTRITION RECORDS. Their
	// Qty_Out equals their Qty_In by construction (saveProductionPhase writes it;
	// the widget never asks for one) and it means "these came back from that
	// operator", not "these were fixed". A garment the operator could not rescue
	// still comes back and is rejected at the NEXT check - that is an inspection
	// outcome, not a production one, and reading it as loss here would book the
	// same garment as lost twice. The stage row answers who did what work, when.
	// It deliberately does not answer how many survived.
	//
	// The arithmetic is declared at the batch; the explanation is attributed at
	// the stage. The damage report the widget opens on a shortfall asks which
	// stage he lost them at, and that answer is allowed to be approximate
	// precisely because nothing computes from it. The number that must be right
	// has one owner and one place to be wrong.
	//
	// ITS OWN FUNCTION, NOT ANOTHER ARGUMENT ON saveProductionPhase. Closing a
	// batch is a different act from closing a stage, and folding it in would give
	// that function's isLastStage a second meaning. Read the comment blocks in
	// saveProductionPhase for what happened the last time something was
	// overloaded onto it.
	//
	// payload:
	// {
	//   "planItemId": "31",
	//   "supervisorId": "5",
	//   "qtyReturning": 28,
	//   "note": ""
	// }
	//
	// returns:
	// {"success":true,"qtyProduced":28,"qtyLost":2,"status":"Awaiting_Check",
	//  "orderVerdict":"..."}
	// or
	// {"success":false,"error":"...","code":"..."}

	// Deluge requires a return at the function's top level, so the result is
	// built into a variable rather than returned from inside the try/catch.
	res = "{\"success\":false,\"error\":\"Nothing was saved\"}";

	// Declared out here so the success line can always reference them, whether
	// or not this close happened to be the one that finished the order.
	verdictTxt = "";
	noteTxt = "";
	supIdTxt = "";

	try
	{
		inp = payloadJson.toMap();
		itemIdStr = ifnull(inp.get("planItemId"),"").toString().trim();
		supIdTxt = ifnull(inp.get("supervisorId"),"").toString().trim();

		// Free text going into hand-built JSON has to lose its quotes or the
		// widget's JSON.parse dies on the response.
		noteTxt = ifnull(inp.get("note"),"").toString().replaceAll("\"","'");

		// Creator and payload numerics that were never written are EMPTY, not
		// null, and toDecimal() throws on empty - normalise to string first.
		qRetStr = ifnull(inp.get("qtyReturning"),"0").toString().trim();
		if(qRetStr == "") { qRetStr = "0"; }

		if(itemIdStr == "" || itemIdStr.isNumber() == false)
		{
			return "{\"success\":false,\"code\":\"BAD_ITEM\",\"error\":\"Missing or non-numeric planItemId\"}";
		}
		if(qRetStr.isNumber() == false)
		{
			return "{\"success\":false,\"code\":\"BAD_QTY\",\"error\":\"qtyReturning is not a number\"}";
		}

		itemId = itemIdStr.toLong();
		qtyReturning = qRetStr.toDecimal().toLong();

		//------------------------------------------------------------------
		// The batch itself.
		//
		// A query returns a COLLECTION, not a record - if(x != null) is true
		// even when nothing matched, so a for-each is the only reliable way to
		// take one record out of one.
		//------------------------------------------------------------------
		itemFound = false;
		itemNameTxt = "";
		statusNow = "";
		qtyOrdered = 0;
		planId = 0;

		for each  piChk in Plan_Item[ID == itemId]
		{
			itemFound = true;
			itemNameTxt = ifnull(piChk.Item_Name,"").toString().replaceAll("\"","'");
			statusNow = ifnull(piChk.Item_Status,"").toString().trim();

			ordS = ifnull(piChk.Qty_Ordered,"0").toString().trim();
			if(ordS == "") { ordS = "0"; }
			qtyOrdered = ordS.toDecimal().toLong();

			// A lookup holds a record id. Forced through string to a long so
			// every comparison below is long-against-long - mixed-type
			// comparison throws, and the id is 18 digits.
			if(piChk.Plan != null)
			{
				planId = piChk.Plan.toString().toLong();
			}
		}

		if(itemFound == false)
		{
			return "{\"success\":false,\"code\":\"NO_ITEM\",\"error\":\"Plan item " + itemId + " not found\"}";
		}

		//------------------------------------------------------------------
		// THIS MUST BE AN ALTERATION BATCH, and Alteration_Stage rows are what
		// make it one.
		//
		// That form exists only for alteration batches - an ordinary item takes
		// its stage list from the BOM, and a batch cannot, because it runs two
		// stages rather than eight. So "has rows" is the test, and there is no
		// flag to keep in sync with it.
		//
		// A plain item coming through here would have its Qty_Produced - the
		// figure its last BOM stage published, which the checker then splits
		// three ways - overwritten by a number somebody typed into a dialog
		// that was never meant for it. This is a Custom API and callable from
		// anywhere, so the widget hiding the button is not the guard.
		//
		// Rows are read in Sequence_No order because the message names them and
		// he thinks of them in the order the work runs. "sort by" on its own
		// assignment - inline in a for-each header it does not reliably parse,
		// and a function that will not save is indistinguishable from one that
		// saved and did nothing.
		//------------------------------------------------------------------
		altRows = Alteration_Stage[Plan_Item == itemId] sort by Sequence_No;

		altCount = 0;
		openCount = 0;
		openNames = "";

		for each  asRow in altRows
		{
			altCount = altCount + 1;

			// Phase_Name is a SNAPSHOT of what the stage was called on the day,
			// and so is Alteration_Stage.Stage_Name - both are Single Line, not
			// a reference. They are matched on exact string, never "contains":
			// the stage names collide as substrings ("Machine Embroidery" sits
			// inside "Manual Machine Embroidery") and a contains match would
			// accept the wrong stage's log as this one's.
			stgName = ifnull(asRow.Stage_Name,"").toString().trim();

			stgDone = false;
			if(stgName != "")
			{
				for each  slChk in Stage_Log[Plan_Item == itemId && Phase_Name == stgName]
				{
					if(ifnull(slChk.Stage_Status,"").toString().trim() == "Done")
					{
						stgDone = true;
					}
				}
			}

			// A stage with no Stage_Log row at all has not been started -
			// Stage_Status has only two values and a row is created at Start,
			// so "no row" is the third state and it belongs on this side.
			if(stgDone == false)
			{
				openCount = openCount + 1;

				if(openNames != "")
				{
					openNames = openNames + ", ";
				}
				openNames = openNames + stgName.replaceAll("\"","'");
			}
		}

		if(altCount == 0)
		{
			return "{\"success\":false,\"code\":\"NOT_ALTERATION\",\"error\":\"" + itemNameTxt + " is not an alteration batch - it has no alteration stages. Ordinary items finish through their last production stage.\"}";
		}

		//------------------------------------------------------------------
		// ALREADY CLOSED FOR GOOD.
		//
		// Complete is reached either through saveItemCheck, which has already
		// split Qty_Produced into accepted and rejected, or through the
		// zero-survivor path below. Rewriting Qty_Produced afterwards would put
		// it at odds with Qty_Accepted with nothing to say which is right, and
		// recheckOrderComplete reads both. Same rule the rest of the app
		// applies: a status that has moved on is not this function's to move
		// back.
		//
		// Awaiting_Check is deliberately NOT refused. The check has not happened
		// yet, so a supervisor correcting the number he declared five minutes
		// ago is honest bookkeeping, and refusing it would leave him no way to
		// fix a typo except a data edit nobody would see.
		//------------------------------------------------------------------
		if(statusNow == "Complete")
		{
			return "{\"success\":false,\"code\":\"BATCH_CLOSED\",\"error\":\"" + itemNameTxt + " is already Complete. Its quantities cannot be re-declared.\"}";
		}

		if(openCount > 0)
		{
			stageWord = "stages are";
			if(openCount == 1) { stageWord = "stage is"; }
			return "{\"success\":false,\"code\":\"STAGES_OPEN\",\"error\":\"" + openCount + " alteration " + stageWord + " still open on " + itemNameTxt + " (" + openNames + "). Finish the work before closing the batch.\"}";
		}

		//------------------------------------------------------------------
		// STILL WAITING ON CLOTH HE ASKED FOR.
		//
		// An alteration batch is created with NO Material_Requirement rows -
		// that is what makes material optional for it. But the reissue path is
		// open to these batches on purpose: "Request material" opens the damage
		// dialog, raiseReissueRequest turns the draft into real requirements
		// with Source = "Alteration", and the batch drops back to
		// Awaiting_Material until the store hands the cloth over.
		//
		// So a batch CAN owe material, and without this guard he would send it
		// to the checker while the store still held a live requirement for it -
		// the garments arriving unfinished, and the requirement outliving the
		// batch that asked for it.
		//
		// THE SAME THREE-PART TEST receiveMaterials USES, and it has to be the
		// same one or the two disagree about whether a row is settled.
		//
		//  1. Received_Qty < Issued_Qty - cloth the store sent that he has not
		//     confirmed. Stock is consumed at RECEIPT; what is in transit is not
		//     on his table.
		//  2. FABRIC IS SATISFIED IN CUT PIECES, NOT METRES. This is the single
		//     most important rule in the app and the one this guard originally
		//     got wrong: comparing Received_Qty against Required_Qty on a fabric
		//     row would have blocked the batch FOR EVER whenever its pieces came
		//     off offcuts, because Required_Qty is a pre-waste metre estimate
		//     that such a row never reaches and is never meant to.
		//  3. Everything else is satisfied in its own unit, on Issued_Qty -
		//     thread, elastic, labels.
		//------------------------------------------------------------------
		matOpen = 0;

		for each  mrRow in Material_Requirement[Plan_Item == itemId]
		{
			reqS = ifnull(mrRow.Required_Qty,"0").toString().trim();
			if(reqS == "") { reqS = "0"; }
			issS = ifnull(mrRow.Issued_Qty,"0").toString().trim();
			if(issS == "") { issS = "0"; }
			recS = ifnull(mrRow.Received_Qty,"0").toString().trim();
			if(recS == "") { recS = "0"; }

			rowOpen = false;

			if(recS.toDecimal() < issS.toDecimal())
			{
				rowOpen = true;
			}

			if(ifnull(mrRow.Is_Fabric,false) == true)
			{
				reqPcsN = ifnull(mrRow.Required_Pieces,0);
				gotPcsN = ifnull(mrRow.Pieces_From_Waste,0) + ifnull(mrRow.Pieces_From_Raw,0);
				if(reqPcsN <= 0 || gotPcsN < reqPcsN)
				{
					rowOpen = true;
				}
			}
			else
			{
				if(issS.toDecimal() < reqS.toDecimal())
				{
					rowOpen = true;
				}
			}

			if(rowOpen == true)
			{
				matOpen = matOpen + 1;
			}
		}

		if(matOpen > 0)
		{
			reqWord = "requests are";
			if(matOpen == 1) { reqWord = "request is"; }
			return "{\"success\":false,\"code\":\"MATERIAL_PENDING\",\"error\":\"" + matOpen + " material " + reqWord + " still not fully received on " + itemNameTxt + ". Receive the material before closing the batch.\"}";
		}

		//------------------------------------------------------------------
		// The declared figure has to be a subset of the batch.
		//
		// More coming back than went out is not a shortfall he can explain - it
		// is garments that belong to some other batch, and letting it through
		// would credit this order with pieces the customer never bought.
		//------------------------------------------------------------------
		if(qtyReturning < 0)
		{
			return "{\"success\":false,\"code\":\"BAD_QTY\",\"error\":\"Cannot return a negative number of garments\"}";
		}
		if(qtyReturning > qtyOrdered)
		{
			return "{\"success\":false,\"code\":\"BAD_QTY\",\"error\":\"Only " + qtyOrdered + " garments went into this batch, so " + qtyReturning + " cannot come back from it.\"}";
		}

		qtyLost = qtyOrdered - qtyReturning;

		//------------------------------------------------------------------
		// THE DECLARATION, and the one place these two numbers are written.
		//
		// NOTHING TO INSPECT goes straight to Complete. A batch that lost every
		// garment has nothing for the checker to look at, and one sitting at
		// Awaiting_Check with zero in it would sit on his queue for ever with
		// no action that could clear it - Awaiting_Check is the queue, so a row
		// on it that cannot be checked is a row nobody can remove.
		//------------------------------------------------------------------
		newStatus = "Awaiting_Check";
		if(qtyReturning == 0)
		{
			newStatus = "Complete";
		}

		for each  piSet in Plan_Item[ID == itemId]
		{
			piSet.Qty_Produced = qtyReturning;
			piSet.Qty_Lost = qtyLost;
			piSet.Item_Status = newStatus;
		}

		//------------------------------------------------------------------
		// Roll the plan up.
		//
		// The PLAN's test is "every item at Awaiting_Check or beyond", not
		// "every item Complete". The plan tracks PRODUCTION, and production on
		// an item is finished the moment it reaches the checker - waiting for
		// the plan to move until every garment has been inspected would leave a
		// finished floor reading as busy for days.
		//
		// Production Complete is the LAST status a plan takes. Checking,
		// packing and dispatch are tracked on Sales_Order.Order_Status from
		// here, which is a different field with its own chain.
		//------------------------------------------------------------------
		if(planId > 0)
		{
			// Read once, up here, because both blocks below need it: the mirror
			// when the plan finishes, and the order recompute when the batch
			// closed empty. Neither is guaranteed to run, so neither can be the
			// one that fetches it.
			orderId = 0;

			for each  plRead in Production_Planning[ID == planId]
			{
				if(plRead.Sales_Order != null)
				{
					orderId = plRead.Sales_Order.toString().toLong();
				}
			}

			allItemsDone = true;
			itemCount = 0;

			for each  piRoll in Plan_Item[Plan == planId]
			{
				itemCount = itemCount + 1;

				rollSt = ifnull(piRoll.Item_Status,"").toString().trim();
				if(rollSt != "Awaiting_Check" && rollSt != "Complete")
				{
					allItemsDone = false;
				}
			}

			if(itemCount > 0 && allItemsDone == true)
			{
				for each  plSet in Production_Planning[ID == planId]
				{
					if(ifnull(plSet.Order_Status,"").toString().trim() != "Production Complete")
					{
						plSet.Order_Status = "Production Complete";
					}
				}

				//----------------------------------------------------------
				// The handover from plan to order. A mirror, not a roll-up:
				// createProductionPlans inserts exactly ONE plan per order
				// inside its per-order loop, so the plan finishing is the
				// order's production finishing.
				//
				// FORWARD ONLY. Every close of every batch on this plan comes
				// back through here, and the all-items-done test stays true
				// once it is true - so an unguarded write would drag an order
				// back from Checking Passed to Production Complete and put it
				// in front of the checker a second time. Same rule
				// saveProductionPhase applies to the order, resolveDispute to
				// the plan and receiveMaterials to Item_Status.
				//----------------------------------------------------------
				if(orderId > 0)
				{
					for each  soSet in Sales_Order[ID == orderId]
					{
						soStNow = ifnull(soSet.Order_Status,"").toString().trim();
						if(soStNow == "Pending" || soStNow == "In Progress")
						{
							soSet.Order_Status = "Production Complete";
						}
					}
				}
			}

			//--------------------------------------------------------------
			// A BATCH THAT CLOSED WITH NOTHING IN IT NEVER REACHES A CHECK, SO
			// NOTHING ELSE WILL EVER RE-ASSESS THE ORDER.
			//
			// Complete is normally arrived at through saveItemCheck, which
			// calls recheckOrderComplete as its last step. This path skips the
			// checker entirely, and if this was the last item of the order the
			// order would hang at Production Complete for ever with nothing on
			// any screen to press. Four callers, one writer - this is one of
			// the four.
			//
			// Called AFTER the roll-up above, not before: recheckOrderComplete
			// only moves an order that is already Production Complete or In
			// Progress, so calling it first would have it look at an order the
			// two lines above were about to move and decide there was nothing
			// to do.
			//
			// It never throws - it swallows its own errors and returns the
			// reason as text - so its result is information, not a condition.
			// The records above are already written and must not be reported
			// as failed because a later step disagreed.
			//--------------------------------------------------------------
			if(qtyReturning == 0)
			{
				if(orderId > 0)
				{
					verdictTxt = thisapp.recheckOrderComplete(orderId.toString()).toString().replaceAll("\"","'");
				}
				else
				{
					verdictTxt = "SKIP: plan " + planId + " has no sales order";
				}
			}
		}

		res = "{\"success\":true,\"qtyProduced\":" + qtyReturning + ",\"qtyLost\":" + qtyLost + ",\"status\":\"" + newStatus + "\",\"orderVerdict\":\"" + verdictTxt + "\"}";
	}
	catch (e)
	{
		res = "{\"success\":false,\"error\":\"DELUGE: " + e.toString().replaceAll("\"","'") + "\"}";
	}

	// THE NOTE HAS NOWHERE ELSE TO GO. Plan_Item carries no remarks field, and
	// the reason for a shortfall is meant to land on the damage report the
	// widget opens next, where it can be attributed to a stage. Logged rather
	// than dropped so an Execute can show what was actually sent, and so a
	// supervisor who typed an explanation here is not silently ignored.
	// supervisorId is logged for the same reason: it is the login stand-in and
	// there is nothing on Plan_Item to record it against.
	info "closeAlterationBatch -> supervisor " + supIdTxt + " | note '" + noteTxt + "' | " + res;

	return res;
}
